<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pula</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pula is a city at the southern tip of the Istrian peninsula in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. As the Roman colony of Pietas Iulia it gained monuments such as its well-preserved amphitheatre of the 1st century CE. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> it was the main base of the imperial navy, and it is now an important port and tourist centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pula.jpg" alt="Pula" width="320" height="195" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orlovic · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pula_Aerial_View.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pula, also called Pola in Italian, is the largest city in Istria County, at the southern tip of the Istrian peninsula in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. It lies on and beneath seven hills, on the inner part of a wide gulf with a naturally well-protected port up to 38 metres deep, which is open to the northwest. It was the administrative centre of Istria from Roman times until Pazin took that role in 1991.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greek tradition attributed the foundation of Polai to Colchians who had chased <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jason">Jason</a> into the northern Adriatic and settled in a place they called the city of refuge. In antiquity the Histri, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetic">Venetic</a> or Illyrian tribe, lived there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> conquered Istria in 177 BCE, and the town was raised to colonial rank between 46 and 45 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. At its height it had about 30,000 people. It took the side of Cassius in the civil war of 42 BCE, and was demolished after Octavian's victory, but was rebuilt at the request of Octavian's daughter Iulia, as Colonia Pietas Iulia Pola Pollentia Herculanea.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> virtually destroyed Pula in 476 CE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> ruled it from 493 to 538. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exarchate-of-ravenna">Exarchate of Ravenna</a>, from 540 to 751, it prospered as the major port of the Byzantine fleet, and the Basilica of Saint Mary Formosa was built in the 6th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> took it in 788. The Venetians took it in 1148 and it swore allegiance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1150, and they ruled it from 1331 until 1797. Plague, malaria and typhoid, along with attacks by Genoese, Hungarian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> forces, left only 3,000 inhabitants by the 1750s.</p><p>After the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1797 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and it became the premier naval port of the Austrian empire in the late 1850s, chosen as the base in 1859 by the Danish admiral Hans Birch Dahlerup. Pola grew from a fading provincial town into an industrial city. In 1910 it had 58,562 inhabitants, 45.8 per cent Italian speaking. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the Istrian Italians of Pula left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in large numbers, and Istria was united with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> on 15 September 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pula is the seventh-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, with a population of 52,220 in 2021. It is known for its ancient Roman buildings, above all the 1st-century amphitheatre, one of the six largest surviving Roman arenas, still used for summer film festivals. The Arch of the Sergii and the Temple of Augustus also survive, and the city is officially bilingual, in Croatian and Italian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rich graves at Nesactium near <b>Pula</b> show a wealthy elite with links to Italy, the Veneti and the Adriatic by the 6th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/histri">Histri</a></li><li><blockquote>The Austro-Hungarian fleet at <b>Pola</b> was handed to it, but Italy seized Istria and parts of Dalmatia under the armistice.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a></li></ul></section>
